    "narrative": "Our strongest offensive outlook centers on **James Wood** (74.28) and **Isaac Paredes** (72.13), both of whom benefit immensely from high-leverage matchups against starting pitchers with xERAs above 4.40. **James Wood** is locked into the leadoff spot against Yesavage, where his elite 0.425 xwOBA combines with favorable park factors to create our highest daily ceiling. Meanwhile, **Isaac Paredes** finds himself in a prime spot against Rodriguez, gaining significant efficiency from a platoon advantage that pushes him into the top tier of our utility rotation. We also see high-end production from **Josh Naylor** (66.08), **Tyler Soderstrom** (64.05), and **Brice Turang** (63.56), all of whom enjoy favorable matchups\u2014specifically against Lauer and Webb\u2014that override minor park or platoon headwinds.\n\nStrategic depth remains crucial today, particularly with the bench decision involving **Edwin Arroyo**. He hovered at a 47.66 score, coming closest to pushing **Dalton Rushing** or **Austin Riley** out of the starting lineup. Ultimately, **Edwin Arroyo** was kept on the bench because his lower lineup position (8th) created a drag on his efficiency that could not match the situational upside of our active starters. We are closely monitoring the 45% rain risk surrounding **Austin Riley** in his match against Scott; should the game face a delay, his below-average 47.75 score makes him the primary candidate to be subbed out. Additionally, we are treading carefully with **Corbin Carroll** (63.20) and **Seiya Suzuki** (53.08), as both face elite defensive catchers who dampen our aggressive stolen base strategy, a critical consideration given our need to prioritize SB efficiency in this 5x5 format.",
